WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.120 --> 00:00:05.680
How did a video game ruin the ending of&nbsp;
“Serenity?” If you’ve come for some of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:10.720
the worst twists in movie history, stick&nbsp;
around, because you’re in the right place!

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:16.880
"Batman Begins" got people to take the Caped&nbsp;
Crusader seriously at the movies again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:22.160
and "The Dark Knight" was an award-winning&nbsp;
sensation. That's why hype was understandably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:28.480
huge for "The Dark Knight Rises" — the final&nbsp;
film in Christopher Nolan's epic Batman trilogy.

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:33.760
Unfortunately, the third film was in many&nbsp;
ways a step down from its predecessors.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:36.320
There are several distracting plot holes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:41.840
such as Bruce Wayne's inexplicable escape to&nbsp;
Gotham after being imprisoned on the other side&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:46.400
of the planet — though his excessive&nbsp;
wealth could be enough explanation.

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:51.600
There's a lot to love about "The Dark Knight&nbsp;
Rises": it's a bombastic thriller filled with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:57.440
excellent visuals and great performances,&nbsp;
especially from Tom Hardy's Bane. The twists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.440 --> 00:01:03.040
weren't great, though. The reveal that Joseph&nbsp;
Gordon-Levitt's character's name is Robin made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:08.640
some people roll their eyes, but it ultimately&nbsp;
didn't affect the plot. However, when Miranda&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:15.440
Tate stabs Batman and reveals herself to be Ra's&nbsp;
al Ghul's daughter Talia — who has Bane on a leash&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:20.960
and is carrying on the legacy of the League&nbsp;
of Shadows — it was a little harder to ignore.

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:25.760
Upon the film's release, some fans complained&nbsp;
that the twist was too predictable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:30.320
while others argued that it undermined&nbsp;
Bane's abilities as a supervillain.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:34.880
Regardless of reasoning, it's generally&nbsp;
agreed that the ending of "The Dark Knight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:39.840
Rises" didn't exactly live up to the&nbsp;
expectations set by its predecessors.

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:46.880
M. Night Shyamalan is a name synonymous with&nbsp;
"twist endings." Shyamalan has had one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:53.520
most bizarre and noteworthy careers in Hollywood&nbsp;
history; "The Sixth Sense," "Unbreakable," and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:59.040
"Signs" were all wildly popular, establishing&nbsp;
him as one of the hottest directors around at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:04.560
the start of the millennium. A decade later,&nbsp;
however, most of the moviegoing public's goodwill&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:09.280
towards him had evaporated thanks to&nbsp;
critical duds like "The Happening,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:15.040
"The Last Airbender," and "After Earth." What&nbsp;
happened between the unforgettable reveal of "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:21.040
Sixth Sense" and the cringe-inducing cheese&nbsp;
of "The Last Airbender" is anyone's guess.

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:24.480
We do know where the first signs of&nbsp;
trouble started popping up, though:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:30.720
2004's "The Village." Released two years after&nbsp;
"Signs," "The Village" follows a group of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:36.800
19th-century Pennsylvanian townsfolk who are&nbsp;
stalked by strange monsters that lurk in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:42.720
woods. Audiences couldn't wait to see what&nbsp;
neck-snapping twist Shyamalan had in store.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:47.040
But when it was revealed that the film&nbsp;
actually takes place in the present day&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:53.360
and that the monsters were just the town elders&nbsp;
in costumes, most people weren't impressed. Still,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:58.320
Shyamalan refuses to let bad reviews&nbsp;
stop him from writing crazy twists;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:04.800
his most recent project, 2021's "Old," features&nbsp;
a similarly baffling reveal of its own.

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:11.040
JK Rowling isn't the only one making things worse&nbsp;
for the legacy of the "Harry Potter" franchise:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:15.840
the other folks behind the "Fantastic&nbsp;
Beasts" movies are helping out too.

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:21.520
The first movie in the spinoff series, in&nbsp;
which British wizard Newt Scamander tries to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.520 --> 00:03:27.200
recapture his escaped magical creatures in&nbsp;
New York City, definitely has its moments:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:31.440
Redmayne charms in the lead&nbsp;
role, his sidekicks are a blast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:36.000
and the various fantastic beasts&nbsp;
certainly live up to the film's title.

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:40.880
However, the movie was never really about&nbsp;
capturing Billywigs and Bowtruckles.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:46.240
Scamander and his friends have to get to the&nbsp;
bottom of an anti-muggle plot orchestrated by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.240 --> 00:03:51.760
Percival Graves, the Director of Magical&nbsp;
Security. When Graves is apprehended,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:58.560
his true identity is revealed: he's actually the&nbsp;
legendary dark wizard Grindlewald in disguise.

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:02.640
That kind of twist might've worked&nbsp;
in a lesser franchise, but the reveal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:07.600
cheapened Grindlewald as a character&nbsp;
moving forward. Worse still, it set up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:14.240
a decidedly unmagical letdown of a sequel in&nbsp;
"Fantastic Beasts: The Crimes of Grindlewald."

00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:20.320
It's no surprise that a film about criminal&nbsp;
magicians who rob the rich and give to the poor is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:25.840
going to have a lot of twists. With a description&nbsp;
like that, the only twist we wouldn't see coming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:31.840
is a straightforward narrative. But of course,&nbsp;
"Now You See Me" has plenty of twists in store.

00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:37.440
The Four Horsemen, a group of expert&nbsp;
illusionists, rob wealthy benefactors and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:44.080
shower their audiences with money. FBI agent&nbsp;
Dylan Rhodes leads the cryptic investigation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:50.320
into their crimes, believing them to be members&nbsp;
of a secret society of magicians known as "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:57.280
Eye." It's certainly a unique idea, and the A-list&nbsp;
cast is an excellent selling point. Unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.280 --> 00:05:03.360
the movie eventually starts to sag under the&nbsp;
weight of all the fakeouts, improbable escapes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:08.640
and double-crosses — but none of them are&nbsp;
nearly as ludicrous as the final reveal:

00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:10.560
"Welcome to The Eye."

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:18.640
Yes, Rhodes, the FBI agent hunting down The&nbsp;
Four Horsemen, is not only a magician himself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.640 --> 00:05:23.840
but the son of another legendary illusionist&nbsp;
and the mysterious mastermind behind the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.840 --> 00:05:30.000
crew's latest epic heist. Far-fetched doesn't&nbsp;
begin to describe this laughable development.

00:05:30.640 --> 00:05:37.680
Like many other movies on this list, "Orphan" has&nbsp;
a promising start. After their child is stillborn,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:43.680
Kate and John Coleman decide to adopt a precocious&nbsp;
nine-year-old girl named Esther [ESS-tur].

00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:50.080
The thing is, Esther is far from a normal kid.&nbsp;
Some problematic behavior might be expected of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.080 --> 00:05:55.920
any child who's waiting for a home in the foster&nbsp;
care system, but Esther is a different kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.920 --> 00:06:01.600
troubled. She interrupts the Colemans during&nbsp;
intimate moments and reveals an alarming level&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.600 --> 00:06:07.520
of knowledge about sexual topics. She scares&nbsp;
the Colemans' other children by killing animals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.520 --> 00:06:13.760
in front of them. Sister Abigail, who runs the&nbsp;
orphanage Esther was adopted from, later informs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.760 --> 00:06:20.640
Kate and John that tragedy always seems to follow&nbsp;
Esther around. Thanks, sister. That would've been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.640 --> 00:06:26.640
nice to know from the beginning. At least she&nbsp;
let them know before Esther was able to kill her.

00:06:26.640 --> 00:06:33.040
Kate is quickly convinced that Esther is sinister,&nbsp;
but it takes John longer to accept the malevolence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.040 --> 00:06:39.360
behind their new daughter's behavior. John finally&nbsp;
admits that something is off when Esther tries to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.360 --> 00:06:45.360
seduce him, but it's pretty much too late at that&nbsp;
point. That's when we learn that Esther isn't nine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:51.840
at all and that she isn't Esther, either: she's&nbsp;
a 33-year-old Estonian woman with a hormone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:58.560
disorder. The bizarre reveal certainly explains a&nbsp;
lot, but that doesn't make it any less ridiculous.

00:06:59.040 --> 00:07:04.240
Christmas movies don't usually wow critics&nbsp;
and are often little more than heartwarming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.240 --> 00:07:08.640
distractions for everyone else. But a&nbsp;
Christmas movie that takes its premise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.640 --> 00:07:13.600
from song lyrics? Now that's a&nbsp;
recipe for narrative disaster.

00:07:13.600 --> 00:07:19.680
Enter "Last Christmas." This story follows Kate,&nbsp;
an aspiring singer who lives with her parents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.680 --> 00:07:24.160
after losing her job and getting thrown&nbsp;
out of her lover's house by his partner.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.160 --> 00:07:29.600
The one bright spot in her life is Tom, a&nbsp;
mysterious man with whom she develops an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.600 --> 00:07:35.840
intimate romance. After the two connect, she&nbsp;
reveals she had a heart transplant a year earlier.

00:07:35.840 --> 00:07:41.680
One day, Kate visits Tom's apartment and&nbsp;
discovers it's being sold. She then learns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.680 --> 00:07:47.440
that the last owner died in a bicycle accident&nbsp;
the previous Christmas. Kate then puts two and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.440 --> 00:07:54.240
two together and realizes — Tom is the previous&nbsp;
owner, and when he died, she received his heart.

00:07:54.240 --> 00:07:57.360
So he was a ghost all along. Great.

00:07:57.360 --> 00:08:02.160
After learning this, Kate tries to focus&nbsp;
on doing good for others, as Tom would've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.160 --> 00:08:08.320
wanted. She spends more time outside and sings&nbsp;
a certain Wham! song at a homeless shelter while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.320 --> 00:08:13.680
reminiscing about the good times she had with&nbsp;
her boyfriend, whom she never actually met.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.680 --> 00:08:19.920
What were the lyrics again? "Last Christmas,&nbsp;
I gave you my heart." Get it? Yeah, we do too.

00:08:20.640 --> 00:08:27.520
John Cusack leads an excellent ensemble cast in&nbsp;
"Identity" — a slasher whodunit very loosely based&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.520 --> 00:08:33.520
on Agatha Christie's "And Then There Were None."&nbsp;
The film follows ten strangers stranded in a hotel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.520 --> 00:08:40.320
who are mysteriously killed off one by one. As&nbsp;
the story progresses, viewers are shown flashbacks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.320 --> 00:08:45.920
depicting important events that supposedly&nbsp;
occurred shortly before the guests arrived.

00:08:45.920 --> 00:08:50.880
The big twist? None of this actually&nbsp;
happened. Malcolm Rivers is a condemned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.880 --> 00:08:56.880
serial killer due to die in less than two&nbsp;
days. His lawyers try to stay the execution,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.880 --> 00:09:02.960
leading to an 11th-hour trial in which it's&nbsp;
revealed that Rivers has Dissociative Identity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.960 --> 00:09:08.800
Disorder. The hotel guest killings represent&nbsp;
Rivers killing his different personalities.

00:09:08.800 --> 00:09:14.560
Twists that are integral to the plot like the&nbsp;
one in "Identity" always work better than twists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.560 --> 00:09:21.280
slapped on at the last minute to shock audiences&nbsp;
and elevate a lousy, boring story. This one is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.280 --> 00:09:26.880
certainly thought-provoking, but ultimately too&nbsp;
absurd and far-fetched to stick the landing.

00:09:28.160 --> 00:09:33.200
"Remember Me" is a sweet but ultimately&nbsp;
forgettable rom-com for most of its runtime.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.200 --> 00:09:39.680
Tyler Hawkins works at the NYU bookstore and&nbsp;
audits classes there while Ally is a student.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.680 --> 00:09:45.680
One night, Tyler and his roommate, Aiden,&nbsp;
are arrested by an NYPD detective named&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.680 --> 00:09:51.200
Neil after getting involved in a fight that&nbsp;
someone else started. When Aiden discovers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.200 --> 00:09:57.520
Neil is Ally's father, he convinces Tyler&nbsp;
to seduce and then dump her to get revenge.

00:09:57.520 --> 00:10:04.560
Instead, Tyler and Ally predictably fall in love&nbsp;
for real after bonding over their shared trauma&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.560 --> 00:10:10.480
and daddy issues. It turns out Tyler's brother&nbsp;
had taken his own life years earlier, leading&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.480 --> 00:10:16.640
his father to focus on raising his sister who&nbsp;
struggles socially and needs support. Meanwhile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.640 --> 00:10:20.960
Ally reveals her mother was mugged&nbsp;
and murdered when she was younger.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.960 --> 00:10:26.960
By this point, some viewers might be rolling their&nbsp;
eyes at all the cheap emotional manipulation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.960 --> 00:10:31.600
which uses extreme tragedy as a&nbsp;
stand-in for more subtle character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.600 --> 00:10:35.440
development — but that's nothing&nbsp;
compared to what's coming next.

00:10:35.440 --> 00:10:40.800
After all of these plot threads have been&nbsp;
resolved, Tyler looks out the window of his new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.800 --> 00:10:47.120
office, located in the north tower of the World&nbsp;
Trade Center ... on the morning of September 11,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.120 --> 00:10:52.000
2001. It doesn't get more&nbsp;
clumsily manipulative than that.

00:10:52.720 --> 00:10:59.360
Not unlike the "Star Wars" prequel trilogy,&nbsp;
the sequels had a rocky time and divided fans.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.360 --> 00:11:03.120
"The Force Awakens" was a&nbsp;
welcome return to form for many,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.120 --> 00:11:09.760
but too similar to "A New Hope" for others. "The&nbsp;
Last Jedi" delighted critics with loads of fresh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.760 --> 00:11:15.520
thought-provoking ideas, but those same risky&nbsp;
storytelling choices left some fans with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.520 --> 00:11:22.320
bad taste in their mouths. And then there's "The&nbsp;
Rise of Skywalker," which pleased almost no one.

00:11:22.320 --> 00:11:26.560
One of Episode 9's many narrative&nbsp;
missteps was how it handled the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.560 --> 00:11:32.240
long-running question of Rey's parentage.&nbsp;
"The Last Jedi" challenged the trope that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.240 --> 00:11:37.440
fantasy protagonists — especially in&nbsp;
"Star Wars" — must have a noble lineage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.440 --> 00:11:42.960
after Kylo Ren tells our heroine that&nbsp;
she's the descendant of "nobodies."

00:11:42.960 --> 00:11:49.200
However, "The Rise of Skywalker" decided to&nbsp;
retcon that development, revealing Rey to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.200 --> 00:11:55.840
a relative of one of the most fearsome figures&nbsp;
in the galaxy far, far away: Emperor Palpatine.

00:11:56.640 --> 00:12:02.080
"You're his granddaughter. You… are a Palpatine."

00:12:03.280 --> 00:12:08.960
The filmmakers behind "The Rise of Skywalker"&nbsp;
were almost certainly trying to recreate the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.960 --> 00:12:15.040
iconic reveal from "The Empire Strikes Back,"&nbsp;
but they were unable to replicate its success.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.040 --> 00:12:20.080
Instead, the sequel trilogy serves as a&nbsp;
cautionary tale of what happens when two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.080 --> 00:12:25.760
filmmakers with opposing visions are forced&nbsp;
to share a multi-billion dollar franchise.

00:12:26.320 --> 00:12:32.560
With the release of both "Iron Man" and "The&nbsp;
Dark Knight," 2008 was a great year for superhero&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.560 --> 00:12:38.880
movies. Unfortunately, the ending of Peter Berg's&nbsp;
"Hancock" meant that they couldn't all be winners.

00:12:38.880 --> 00:12:45.760
Will Smith plays the titular Hancock, an abrasive,&nbsp;
alcoholic superhuman who can't remember his own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.760 --> 00:12:52.720
secret identity. Jason Bateman plays Ray Embrey,&nbsp;
a PR specialist who volunteers to help Hancock&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.720 --> 00:12:59.040
revitalize his image after the hero saves his&nbsp;
life. With that unique and seemingly fun premise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.040 --> 00:13:04.320
the pieces were all there for a recommendable&nbsp;
one-off action-comedy superhero flick.

00:13:04.320 --> 00:13:07.760
But then the second half of the&nbsp;
movie happens, and we find out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.760 --> 00:13:13.920
Ray's wife is also an immortal superhuman.&nbsp;
And she's also Hancock's ex-wife. It turns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.920 --> 00:13:18.800
out that superheroes only have powers when&nbsp;
they're apart, and his proximity to her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.800 --> 00:13:25.200
made him vulnerable to an attack 80 years ago,&nbsp;
resulting in his hospitalization and amnesia.

00:13:25.200 --> 00:13:31.360
Viewers generally agreed that "Hancock's" two&nbsp;
halves felt disjointed — which isn't a surprise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.360 --> 00:13:35.200
considering that the film was stuck&nbsp;
in development hell for 12 years.

00:13:35.840 --> 00:13:41.760
Matthew McConaughey and Anne Hathaway had perfect&nbsp;
chemistry in "Interstellar," so it was a bit of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.760 --> 00:13:48.480
surprise that 2019's "Serenity" missed the mark as&nbsp;
much as it did. The plot sounds intriguing enough,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:48.480 --> 00:13:54.880
at least on paper: boat captain Dill Baker is&nbsp;
asked by his ex-wife Karen to save her and her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:54.880 --> 00:14:01.280
son Patrick by murdering her abusive husband,&nbsp;
Frank. She books Frank for a ride on Dill's boat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:01.280 --> 00:14:07.040
for a week, where it should be easy to toss him&nbsp;
overboard with no witnesses. But Dill is forced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:07.040 --> 00:14:12.080
to wrestle with his conscience over whether&nbsp;
or not he should go through with it after all.

00:14:12.080 --> 00:14:17.840
So what's the big twist? Does Karen have a hidden&nbsp;
agenda? Has Frank been dead the whole time?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:17.840 --> 00:14:24.480
Is Dill secretly Grindlewald in disguise?&nbsp;
No, no, and no. It turns out that Dill is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.480 --> 00:14:30.080
a playable video game character designed&nbsp;
by Patrick. Only after Dill completes his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:30.080 --> 00:14:35.360
in-game objective does Patrick work up&nbsp;
the courage to kill Frank in real life.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:35.360 --> 00:14:41.280
In his review of the film for The New York Times,&nbsp;
critic Wesley Morris said of the big reveal,

00:14:41.280 --> 00:14:43.840
"[It's] like the worst of Christopher Nolan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:43.840 --> 00:14:48.560
M. Night Shyamalan and the&nbsp;
condolence-card section at Walgreens."

